0486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420493 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420493 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420493 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420493 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc977685-7a6f-5892-9d86-65a9ccbd87da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-226151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/de3e018fa1382ee2250d1b2e256e3b47cafef346852007f0e6afb84342d7cb04.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6071adb1-29d3-5cda-a599-699b41d64b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420496 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477605 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477606 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477607 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1a7556c-36af-5f0b-b64c-a00089e6850e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420504 #edb/ref 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=2\\cdot 0-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=2>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ then $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921864